You can do this safely by exposing an HTTPS endpoint on the server and having each client periodically POST status data (e.g., CPU, disk, uptime) over TLS.

Here’s a minimal, security‑conscious example.

1. Nginx configuration (server side)

Assuming you’ve already obtained a TLS certificate (via Let’s Encrypt or similar) and placed it at the indicated paths:

```nginx
http {
    # Basic log format
    log_format status_logs '$remote_addr - $time_local "$request" '
                         '$status $body_bytes_sent "$http_user_agent"';

    server {
        listen 443 ssl;
        server_name status.example.com;

        ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/status.example.com/fullchain.pem;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/status.example.com/privkey.pem;

        # Only allow POST to /report
        location /report {
            limit_except POST { deny all; }

            # Optional simple auth token check
            if ($http_x_auth_token != "SUPER_SECRET_TOKEN") {
                return 403;
            }

            # Log the body to a file via access_log
            access_log /var/log/nginx/status_reports.log status_logs;

            # Discard body (we only log the request)
            proxy_pass_request_body off;
            proxy_set_header Content-Length "0";
            return 204;
        }
    }
}
```

This config:
- Listens on HTTPS
- Accepts only POST requests to `/report`
- Optionally checks a simple header token
- Logs incoming requests for later analysis

For more advanced setups, you could proxy to a backend (e.g., a Python/Go service) that parses and stores JSON payloads.

2. Bash one‑liner for clients (Linux machines)

This example sends a small JSON payload with some status info using `curl` over HTTPS and an auth header:

```bash
STATUS_JSON='{"host":"'"$(hostname)"'","uptime":"'"$(uptime -p)"'","loadavg":"'"$(cat /proc/loadavg | cut -d" " -f1-3)"'"}'

curl -sS -X POST \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -H 'X-Auth-Token: SUPER_SECRET_TOKEN' \
  --data "$STATUS_JSON" \
  https://status.example.com/report
```

To run this periodically, you could add a cron job such as:

```bash
*/5 * * * * /usr/bin/env bash -c 'STATUS_JSON="{\"host\":\"$(hostname)\",\"uptime\":\"$(uptime -p)\"}"; curl -sS -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -H "X-Auth-Token: SUPER_SECRET_TOKEN" --data "$STATUS_JSON" https://status.example.com/report >/dev/null 2>&1'
```

This pattern is appropriate for legitimate monitoring and management of systems you control, while respecting security and legal boundaries.

